          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why is it hard to keep taking a preventer inhaler every day when you feel fine?",
          "answer": "The guidance I could search does not directly explain why people find it hard to take a preventer inhaler when they feel well. The only asthma passage I found is from \"Sticking with asthma treatment: inhaler technique, controllers and action plans.\" It focuses on how the inhaler is used, not on the emotional or habit side of daily use.\n\nThat passage says poor inhaler technique is common, can last for years, and is often the first thing checked when treatment seems to be failing. It also says the problem depends on the device. Pressurized inhalers often go wrong with poor timing between pressing and breathing in, or breathing in too fast. Dry powder inhalers need a fast, deep breath, and breathing out into them can ruin the dose. Someone who uses both types may be asked to do two contradictory things.\n\nA diabetes document (\"Sticking with a diabetes regimen,\" from a national diabetes association summary) offers some general habits that may apply to any daily medicine. It suggests tying doses to an existing routine, using a pill organizer or reminders, and asking directly about cost, since people often skip doses because of cost without mentioning it. It is not about asthma, so treat it as general background only.\n\nThe sources do not explain why feeling well makes people stop, so please ask your clinician or pharmacist about that. Don't stop or change an inhaler on your own. They can check your technique and talk through any difficulty with the routine.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
